  q(COMM,'A manager invites staff to come in any time to share concerns. What is this practice called?','An open-door policy',['A chain of command','The grapevine','A formal grievance hearing'],'D','Open-door policies support upward communication and help managers spot problems early.',BUCH),
  q(COMM,'A hospital leader holds a large meeting where all staff can hear updates and ask questions. What is this called?','A town hall meeting',['A huddle','A debrief','A performance review'],'A','Town halls show transparency and let leaders answer concerns directly.',BUCH),
  q(COMM,'Staff receive so many emails and alerts that they miss important messages. Which barrier is this?','Information overload',['Filtering','Selective perception','Semantic noise from jargon'],'B','Managers can reduce overload by sending fewer, clearer messages and marking priorities.',SK),
  q(QUAL,'A hospital compares its infection rates with those of top-performing hospitals to find ways to improve. What is this?','Benchmarking',['Root cause analysis','Brainstorming','Failure mode and effects analysis'],'C','Benchmarking helps organizations set realistic goals based on best practices.',SK),
  q(QUAL,'The Institute for Healthcare Improvement’s Triple Aim seeks better patient experience, better population health and:','Lower per capita cost',['More hospital beds','Higher prices','More specialists'],'D','Some groups add a fourth aim: improving the work life of health care staff.',SK),
  q(QUAL,'Which WHO tool has surgical teams pause at set points to confirm the patient, site and procedure?','The Surgical Safety Checklist',['The Pareto chart','The balanced scorecard','The SWOT analysis'],'A','Using the checklist has been linked to fewer complications and deaths after surgery.',SK),
  q(QUAL,'Which independent group gives U.S. hospitals letter grades for patient safety?','The Leapfrog Group',['The FDA','The AMA','The American Red Cross'],'B','Leapfrog Hospital Safety Grades are based on errors, infections and injuries.',BUCH),
  q(QUAL,'Which chart plots pairs of values to show whether two variables are related, such as staffing and falls?','A scatter diagram',['A Pareto chart','A fishbone diagram','A flowchart'],'C','A pattern in the points suggests a relationship, but it does not prove cause and effect.',SK),
  q(STRAT,'A clinic serves only one market segment, such as sports medicine for athletes. Which strategy is this?','Focus strategy',['Cost leadership','Retrenchment','Vertical integration of suppliers'],'D','A focus strategy targets a narrow group with services tailored to its needs.',SK),
  q(STRAT,'A hospital compares where it is now with where it wants to be and lists what is missing. What is this?','A gap analysis',['A break-even analysis','A Pareto analysis','A cost-benefit study of one drug'],'A','Gap analysis guides plans to close the distance between current and desired performance.',BUCH),
  q(STRAT,'A $200,000 investment produces $250,000 in returns. What is the return on investment (ROI)?','25%',['20%','50%','125%'],'B','ROI = (return − cost) ÷ cost = $50,000 ÷ $200,000 = 25%, a gain of 25 cents per dollar invested.',BUCH),
  q(STRAT,'Leaders plan how they would respond to several possible futures, such as a new competitor or a payment cut. What is this?','Scenario planning',['Budgeting','A SWOT analysis','An operational plan'],'C','Scenario planning helps organizations stay ready when the future is uncertain.',SK),
  q(STRAT,'A hospital opens a fitness center and a retail pharmacy, both outside its usual services. Which growth strategy is this?','Diversification',['Market penetration','Retrenchment','Cost leadership'],'D','Diversification spreads risk across different lines of business.',SK),
  q(POL,'Which part of Medicare mainly covers physician visits and outpatient services?','Part B',['Part A','Part C','Part D'],'A','Part B has a monthly premium, while most people do not pay a premium for Part A.',BUCH),
  q(POL,'Which federal law, effective in 2022, protects patients from most surprise bills for out-of-network emergency care?','The No Surprises Act',['HIPAA','EMTALA','The Stark Law'],'B','The law also requires good faith cost estimates for uninsured or self-pay patients.',BUCH),
  q(POL,'Which federal program requires drug makers to sell outpatient drugs at discounts to safety-net hospitals and clinics?','The 340B Drug Pricing Program',['Medicare Part D','CHIP','The Hill-Burton program'],'C','Savings are meant to help these providers serve more low-income and uninsured patients.',BUCH),
  q(POL,'Groups of providers that share savings with Medicare when they lower costs while meeting quality goals are called:','Accountable care organizations (ACOs)',['Health maintenance organizations','Critical access hospitals','Retail clinics'],'D','ACOs are a value-based model that rewards providers for coordinated, efficient care.',BUCH),
  q(POL,'Which federal agency sets rules to protect health care workers from bloodborne pathogens such as HIV?','OSHA',['CMS','FDA','FTC'],'A','OSHA’s Bloodborne Pathogens Standard requires training, safety devices and hepatitis B vaccine offers.',BUCH),
  q(CONS,'To keep their tax exemption, nonprofit hospitals must show they provide what?','Community benefit, such as charity care',['Profits to shareholders','The lowest prices in the state','Care only to insured patients'],'B','Community benefit includes free care, health education and research that serves the public.',BUCH),
  q(CONS,'Clinics inside pharmacies or stores that treat minor illnesses with walk-in visits are called:','Retail clinics',['Critical access hospitals','Skilled nursing facilities','Ambulatory surgery centers'],'C','Retail clinics appeal to consumers who want convenience, short waits and clear prices.',BUCH),
  q(CONS,'Which ethical principle means keeping promises and being loyal to commitments made to patients?','Fidelity',['Justice','Autonomy','Nonmaleficence'],'D','Fidelity builds trust between patients and health care organizations.',BUCH),
  q(CONS,'A patient and clinician review options together, considering evidence and the patient’s values, before choosing a treatment. What is this?','Shared decision-making',['Paternalism','Defensive medicine','Informed refusal'],'A','Decision aids, such as videos or charts, help patients compare benefits and risks.',BUCH),
  q(CONS,'A patient with a terminal illness asks not to receive CPR if their heart stops. Which order documents this?','A do-not-resuscitate (DNR) order',['A living will','A power of attorney','A discharge order'],'B','A DNR is a physician’s order, while a living will is a document written by the patient.',BUCH)
